  "question": {
    "text": "The angle of incidence at which reflected light totally polarized for reflection from air to glass (refractive index $n$ ), is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "TiEwA",
      "text": "\n$\\sin ^{-1}(n)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "pHhFd",
      "text": "\n$\\sin ^{-1}(1 / n)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "P1TAs",
      "text": "\n$\\tan ^{-1}(1 / n)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "q3xVd",
      "text": "\n$\\tan ^{-1}(n)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Brewster's law: According to this law the ordinary light is completely polarised in the plane of incidence when it gets reflected from transparent medium at a particular angle known as the angle of polarisation.\n$$\n\\mathrm{n}=\\tan \\mathrm{i}_{\\mathrm{p}}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
